<section id="comments">
  <h2>Comments (9)</h2>

  
    <a id="comment-1191" class="header-offset"></a>
    <article>
      <header>
        <p><a href="/tag/00NX#comment-1191">Comment #1191</a>
        by
        <cite>Lenny Taelman</cite>
        on <time datetime="2014-12-05 10:47:32">December 05, 2014 at 10:47 </time>
      </header>

      <blockquote><p>In (1) ==&gt; (7): "The corresponding surjection" is to M_g, not M_g^r.</p></blockquote>
    </article>
  
    <a id="comment-1205" class="header-offset"></a>
    <article>
      <header>
        <p><a href="/tag/00NX#comment-1205">Comment #1205</a>
        by
        <cite><a href="http://math.columbia.edu/~dejong" rel="author">Johan</a></cite>
        
        on <time datetime="2014-12-08 14:14:38">December 08, 2014 at 14:14 </time>
      </header>

      <blockquote><p>Thanks! See <a href="https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/commit/d1c1d314bdb597e7224bbed6890091edf41d3f10">here</a>.</p></blockquote>
    </article>
  
    <a id="comment-6248" class="header-offset"></a>
    <article>
      <header>
        <p><a href="/tag/00NX#comment-6248">Comment #6248</a>
        by
        <cite>Rein Janssen Groesbeek</cite>
        on <time datetime="2021-05-19 08:09:20.813824">May 19, 2021 at 08:09 </time>
      </header>

      <blockquote><p>
<script type="math/tex">\def\sb{_}</script> In (1) <script type="math/tex">\Rightarrow</script> (7), in the first application of Nakayama, I think it is applied on <script type="math/tex">M\sb{\mathfrak{p}}</script> instead of <script type="math/tex">M</script>, as <script type="math/tex">M \otimes\sb{R} \kappa(\mathfrak{p})</script> is a quotient of <script type="math/tex">M\sb{\mathfrak{p}}</script> and not of <script type="math/tex">M</script> if <script type="math/tex">\mathfrak{p}</script> is not maximal.
So Nakayama's Lemma gives <script type="math/tex">\operatorname{Coker}(R^{\oplus r} \xrightarrow{x_i} M)\sb{\mathfrak{p}} = 0</script>, and the existence of <script type="math/tex">g \in R, g \not \in \mathfrak{p}</script> such that <script type="math/tex">x_1, \dots, x_r</script> generate <script type="math/tex">M_g</script> follows from finiteness of <script type="math/tex">\operatorname{Coker}(R^{\oplus r} \xrightarrow{x_i} M)</script>.<br>
Similarly in the second application, Nakayama gives <script type="math/tex">\operatorname{Ker}(\varphi)\sb{\mathfrak{p}} =0</script> and the existence of <script type="math/tex">g' \in R\sb{g}</script> follows from finiteness of <script type="math/tex">\operatorname{Ker}(\varphi)</script>.</p></blockquote>
    </article>
  
    <a id="comment-6380" class="header-offset"></a>
    <article>
      <header>
        <p><a href="/tag/00NX#comment-6380">Comment #6380</a>
        by
        <cite><a href="https://math.columbia.edu/~dejong" rel="author">Johan</a></cite>
        
        on <time datetime="2021-07-18 11:57:03.094350">July 18, 2021 at 11:57 </time>
      </header>

      <blockquote><p>THanks and fixed <a href="https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/commit/ee16ab359afde37a99a11dd9408554a9ee0a64bf">here</a>.</p></blockquote>
    </article>
  
    <a id="comment-6382" class="header-offset"></a>
    <article>
      <header>
        <p><a href="/tag/00NX#comment-6382">Comment #6382</a>
        by
        <cite>Laurent Moret-Bailly</cite>
        on <time datetime="2021-07-18 14:13:49.260176">July 18, 2021 at 14:13 </time>
      </header>

      <blockquote><p>Condition (7) looks incomplete.</p></blockquote>
    </article>
  
    <a id="comment-6384" class="header-offset"></a>
    <article>
      <header>
        <p><a href="/tag/00NX#comment-6384">Comment #6384</a>
        by
        <cite><a href="https://math.columbia.edu/~dejong" rel="author">Johan</a></cite>
        
        on <time datetime="2021-07-18 14:44:06.781494">July 18, 2021 at 14:44 </time>
      </header>

      <blockquote><p>@#6382. Sorry, can you clarify?</p></blockquote>
    </article>
  
    <a id="comment-6392" class="header-offset"></a>
    <article>
      <header>
        <p><a href="/tag/00NX#comment-6392">Comment #6392</a>
        by
        <cite>Laurent Moret-Bailly</cite>
        on <time datetime="2021-07-19 02:37:52.023229">July 19, 2021 at 02:37 </time>
      </header>

      <blockquote><p>@#6384: I had not realized that "and" might announce the last condition, so I read it as an unfinished sentence. Another thing: the difference between (6) and (7) will be unclear to those reading this lemma without seeing Definition 00NW first. </p></blockquote>
    </article>
  
    <a id="comment-11434" class="header-offset"></a>
    <article>
      <header>
        <p><a href="/tag/00NX#comment-11434">Comment #11434</a>
        by
        <cite>Jiamin Pan</cite>
        on <time datetime="2026-05-14 22:07:51.007377">May 14, 2026 at 22:07 </time>
      </header>

      <blockquote><p>In the proof of the direction (8) <script type="math/tex">\Rightarrow</script> (7), the citation for "In particular <script type="math/tex">\rho_M(m)=r</script>. By Nakayama's Lemma 10.20.1 there exists ..." does not seem to be correct. The statements for Nakayama's Lemma there does not give the desired implication. Instead, one should refer to Lemma 10.20.2 \href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GLX} with <script type="math/tex">I:=0</script>, which ensures the existence of the desired element <script type="math/tex">f</script>. </p></blockquote>
    </article>
  
    <a id="comment-11539" class="header-offset"></a>
    <article>
      <header>
        <p><a href="/tag/00NX#comment-11539">Comment #11539</a>
        by
        <cite><a href="https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/" rel="author">Stacks Project</a></cite>
        
        on <time datetime="2026-05-29 16:08:12.575965">May 29, 2026 at 16:08 </time>
      </header>

      <blockquote><p>I do think that part (7) of Nakayama's lemma gives exactly the statement we're looking for. Leaving as is.</p></blockquote>
    </article>
  

  
    <p>There are also:
    <ul>
      
        <li>4 comment(s) on Section <a href="/tag/00NV#comments" data-tag="00NV">10.78</a>: Finite projective modules
      
    </ul>
  
